  "course": "statistics-economics",
  "title": "Statistics for Economics",
  "description": "Original constructed probability and statistics exercises preparing students for econometrics without duplicating regression and causal-design drills. Prerequisites: algebra, elementary functions, summation notation and basic single-variable calculus; derivatives support likelihood and allocation extensions. Probability is developed within the course. Advanced bridge items distinguish laws of large numbers, central limit theorems, heavy tails, likelihood support, normal pivots, conditional bootstrap distributions and statistical decision loss. All data and numerical scenarios are hypothetical, and finite-sample versus asymptotic assumptions are stated in the questions.",
